    {
      "paper_id": "doi:10.1086/722394",
      "sequence": 2,
      "source_sequence": 1249,
      "article_type": "research-article",
      "title_en": "US Inequality and Fiscal Progressivity: An Intragenerational Accounting",
      "title_cn": "美国不平等与财政累进性：代内核算",
      "authors": [
        "Alan J. Auerbach",
        "Laurence J. Kotlikoff",
        "Darryl Koehler"
      ],
      "abstract_en": "This study measures spending power inequality within age cohorts and estimates fiscal progressivity via lifetime net tax rates. We find, first, that inequality in income and especially wealth dramatically overstates inequality in spending power. Second, inequality in current spending power differs from that in lifetime spending power because of credit constraints, in-kind government benefits, and other factors. Third, the US fiscal system is highly progressive once cohorts are old enough to have highly dispersed human wealth. Fourth, households’ rankings based on current income can differ substantially from their rankings based on lifetime resources. Fifth, current-year net tax rates substantially understate fiscal progressivity.",
      "abstract_cn": "本研究衡量了同一年龄队列内的消费能力不平等，并通过终身净税率估算财政累进性。我们发现，首先，收入尤其是财富的不平等严重夸大了消费能力的不平等。其次，由于信贷约束、实物政府福利及其他因素，当前消费能力的不平等与终身消费能力的不平等存在差异。第三，一旦队列年龄足够大，其人力财富高度分散，美国财政体系便具有高度累进性。第四，基于当前收入的家庭排名可能与其基于终身资源的排名存在显著差异。第五，当年净税率大幅低估了财政累进性。",
